    "content": "Uganda's Ministry of Health has formally announced that the country is free of Ebola, after completing 42 days—two full incubation periods—with no new confirmed cases. This milestone meets international standards set by the World Health Organization for declaring an end to an Ebola outbreak.\n\nThe outbreak, which began in mid-May 2026, resulted in 20 confirmed cases and two deaths. Health officials have credited rapid response teams, contact tracing, and community engagement for containing the virus. No new infections have been recorded since late June.\n\nHealth authorities maintained strict surveillance throughout the 42-day period, monitoring all contacts of confirmed patients. The end of the outbreak brings relief to Uganda's healthcare system, which had mobilized significant resources to combat the disease.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom have family or business ties in Uganda, the declaration means reduced travel restrictions and easing of screening measures at borders. It also underscores the importance of continued investment in public health systems across East Africa to prevent and swiftly contain future outbreaks.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/07/28/uganda-oo-si-rasmi-ah-ugu-dhawaaqay-inuu-dalkeeda-ka-baxay-cudurka-ebola/",
