    "content": "Former Somali Prime Minister Omar Abdirashid Ali Sharmarke has issued a warning to the Puntland administration regarding the possibility of armed conflict in the city of Galkayo. In a statement, he called on all parties to exercise restraint and prioritize peaceful resolution of disputes. Galkayo, which straddles the boundary between Puntland and Galmudug, has historically been a flashpoint for clashes between the two federal member states.\n\nSharmarke, who served as prime minister from 2009 to 2010 and again in 2014-2017, emphasized that any military confrontation would have devastating consequences for civilians and undermine regional stability. His comments come amid heightened tensions following recent disagreements over control of the city's districts. The former premier urged both Puntland and Galmudug leaders to engage in direct talks rather than resort to force.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, stability in Galkayo is of particular concern because many diaspora members originate from the Mudug region and maintain family and business ties there. Past conflicts have disrupted trade routes and humanitarian access, affecting remittances and investment flows. Remittances from diaspora Somalis are a critical economic lifeline for many families in the area.\n\nThe international community, including the African Union and United Nations, has also called for de-escalation. Sharmarke's intervention underscores the risk of relapse into clan-based violence, which could reverse security gains made over the past decade. Analysts view Galkayo as a test case for federal-state cooperation in Somalia.\n\nAs of now, no formal dialogue has been announced, but local elders are working to mediate between the sides. The situation remains fluid, with many hoping that leaders will heed Sharmarke's call for peace.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/cumar-cabdirashiid-oo-ka-digey-colaad-ka-dhacda-galkacyo/424224/",
